In article <5hbd2.1942$Bc5.4333@news.flash.net>,
j baker <jb@nowhere.com> wrote:

>Bad application might not be killing its children when it terminates.

If the parent process is gone, there's no way for child processes
to become zombies.  If they still run, they're normal processes,
if they die, they get adopted by init which properly waits for their
termination.  Zombies only occur when children have died and the parent
process has not yet acknowledged their death (so that the system has
to keep an entry in the process table which contains the exit code,
resource usage values etc.).

-- 
